# Choice of a function variant with subsorts (SUBSORT): a variant that fits
# exactly comes first, conversions are the fallback, and the variant with
# the fewest conversions wins (like the most specific method in Julia).
INC "inc/all"
# real < complex (inc/alg): complex functions also take real numbers
Re(2.5)
Im(3)
1 + (im + 3)
# precedence: the variant for real fits exactly, the complex one is the
# fallback for complex arguments
FUNC tst : complex >> real ATT c_real
OTHER tst : real >> real ATT r_abs
tst(-2.5)
tst(-2.5+im)
# rational -> real (1 conversion) beats rational -> real -> complex (2)
tst(-5/2)
# exact numbers: natural is a subsort of integer and of nonneg; inc/exact
# gives explicit variants where both would fit equally well
7/2
2^(1/2)
2^-1
# a tie that is not resolved: reported once, the first variant is used
FUNC amb : integer >> integer ATT int_abs
OTHER amb : nonneg >> nonneg ATT r_sqrt
amb(4)
amb(9)
